"expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4530638057,"indicator":"usa.on1xvpn.ru","type":"hostname","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:44","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["ultraviewer","detour dog","cloudflare pages","roi777","hotejka","balada injector","tech support scam","help tds"],"targeted_countries":["United States of America","Australia","Belgium","Canada","France","Japan","Sweden"],"malware_families":["UltraViewer"],"attack_ids":["T1132.001","T1036.005","T1566.002","T1598.003","T1608.001","T1219","T1583.001","T1185","T1036.004","T1102","T1583.006","T1204","T1566","T1110","T1078","T1056","T1189","T1134","T1071.001","T1584.004"],"references":["https:\/\/www.infoblox.com\/blog\/threat-intelligence\/2026\/10\/inside-help-tds-hotejka-cloudflare-pages-and-predictive-detection"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6aedb6e121254ba434499c9a","name":"Lookalike Domains Expose the iPhone Theft Economy","description":"A thriving underground marketplace operating primarily through Telegram has emerged to unlock stolen iPhones, making phone theft profitable despite Apple's security features like Activation Lock. Thieves combine technical tools and social engineering through SMS phishing campaigns using lookalike domains to trick legitimate owners into surrendering their passcodes. These unlocking tools, sold on pay-as-you-go basis for $5-50, can jailbreak older phones and extract information for crafting credible phishing attacks. Analysis reveals over 10,000 domains associated with these operations, with DNS traffic increasing 350% in 2025. Sellers operate globally from countries including Bangladesh, India, Pakistan, Venezuela, Mexico, and Brazil. The ecosystem prioritizes wiping devices for quick resale rather than data theft, with unlocked high-end phones fetching hundreds of dollars on black markets.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-06T19:02:41.730000","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:45.531000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4566479925,"indicator":"apple.com-app.lt","type":"hostname","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:46","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4566479926,"indicator":"apple.connect-app.info","type":"hostname","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:46","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4566479927,"indicator":"find.my-id.com.es","type":"hostname","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:46","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4566479928,"indicator":"phone.xuidns.pw","type":"hostname","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:46","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["device unlock","unlocking tools","phishing kits","smishing","iphone theft","telegram marketplace","social engineering","lookalike domains"],"targeted_countries":["United States of America"],"malware_families":[],"attack_ids":["T1036.005","T1566.002","T1598.003","T1566.001","T1608.001","T1583.001","T1036","T1589.002","T1589.003","T1070","T1204","T1534","T1566","T1078","T1589.001","T1598","T1496","T1070.004","T1204.001","T1078.004","T1566.003"],"references":["https:\/\/www.infoblox.com\/blog\/threat-intelligence\/lookalike-domains-expose-the-iphone-theft-economy?utm_campaign=ww-ems-q4fy26-secnet-monthlyproductupdate-20260616&utm_medium=email-sales&utm_source=sales&utm_term=na&utm_content=na"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6aedb6df481d077141e5e5e4","name":"Remcos RAT","description":"This intelligence analysis details a malicious campaign involving Remcos RAT, a commercially available remote access trojan. The attack chain begins with a weaponized Excel spreadsheet (Remittance Advice.xlsm) that executes VBA macros. Upon opening, PowerShell retrieves remote scripts (edi.vbs\/firewall.vbs) from a malicious domain. The execution progresses through multiple stages, ultimately deploying the Remcos RAT payload. Command and control infrastructure utilizes domains at dvrlists[.]com and lbl[.]support. The threat demonstrates typical social engineering tactics using financial-themed lures to entice victims into enabling macros and initiating the infection chain.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-06T18:59:55.886000","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:43.551000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4539484199,"indicator":"CVE-2026-109618","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:44","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4539484200,"indicator":"CVE-2026-109619","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:44","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4566479904,"indicator":"50f2ff7d96392fcfe6ed57a1ff71ae9c87a1346ff3694173a255b67e9ff8a208","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:44","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4566479905,"indicator":"abf8ada022fce92c24e0aead4f1b1ae8991002130bbbc4335f3381972683b400","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:44","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4566479906,"indicator":"http:\/\/lbl.support","type":"URL","created":"2026-11-06T11:15:44","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["powershell","excel weaponization","remcos rat","remote access trojan","command and control","remcos","vba macros","social engineering"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":["Remcos"],"attack_ids":["T1113","T1056.001","T1204.002","T1566.001","T1082","T1219","T1055","T1057","T1059.001","T1027","T1071.001","T1059.005","T1105"],"references":["https:\/\/www.esentire.com\/blog\/remcos-rat"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6ac679e7bbe47190f9ffce7a","name":"FortiBleed Operations Continue Targeting Exposed Systems Leading to Reports of Lockouts","description":"FortiBleed is an active global credential-compromise campaign targeting internet-facing Fortinet FortiGate firewalls and SSL VPN gateways, with over 86,644 compromised devices verified across 194 countries. The campaign exploits reused or leaked credentials and legacy SHA-256 password storage, enabling threat actors to harvest and crack authentication data at scale using distributed GPU clusters. Attackers scan for exposed FortiGate SSL VPN portals, collect credentials through stuffing and password spraying, and crack stolen password hashes offline. Once access is gained, they create new administrative accounts for persistence and conduct Active Directory enumeration to expand access. Affected organizations may experience lockouts as threat actors delete or modify existing accounts. The operation functions as an initial-access broker, with connections observed to INC\/Lynx and Payload ransomware affiliates, representing a significant threat requiring immediate defensive action.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-06T16:03:39.952000","created":"2026-10-07T16:57:11.669000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[],"tags":["fortigate","credential harvesting","hashtopolis","fortibleed","gpu cracking","inc\/lynx","fortinet","payload","initial access broker","ssl vpn"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":["INC\/Lynx","Payload"],"attack_ids":["T1583","T1003","T1110.002","T1087.002","T1190","T1136.001","T1595","T1110.003","T1087","T1041","T1078","T1531","T1110.004"],"references":["https:\/\/www.ic3.gov\/CSA\/2026\/261006.pdf"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"6aed32c8b1c53b811aaa8bb0","name":"A Dish Served Lukewarm: The Refrigerator Botnet","description":"Exposed Dixell\/Copeland XWEB PRO refrigeration controllers are being exploited as abuse infrastructure. The STRIKE Team discovered a suspected SMTP botnet comprising nine IP addresses, with three confirmed as refrigeration controllers. These devices, used in supermarkets and cold storage facilities, remain exposed on the internet with unpatched vulnerabilities, particularly CVE-2026-25085. Attackers are repurposing these controllers as tunnel gateways, residential-proxy exits, and spam senders. Analysis revealed outbound-only SMTP traffic with significant target-list overlap among nodes, indicating coordinated operations. Of 1,041 mail targets, 508 were hit by multiple nodes, with the three most active sharing approximately 47% of their targets. The exposed controllers carry vulnerable firmware and lack security monitoring, making them ideal for prolonged malicious use without detection.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-11-06T10:46:18.670000","created":"2026-11-06T01:52:08.470000","revision":3,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":3860907229,"indicator":"84.1.34.242","type":"IPv4","created":"2026-11-06T01:52:09","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":"2026-12-06T01:00:00","is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4021694418,"indicator":"78.21.151.21","type":"IPv4","created":"2026-11-06T01:52:09","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":"2026-12-06T01:00:00","is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4021708020,"indicator":"90.0.156.128","type":"IPv4","created":"2026-11-06T01:52:09","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":"2026-12-06T01:00:00","is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4228222010,"indicator":"CVE-2026-25085","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-06T01:52:09","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4539484199,"indicator":"CVE-2026-109618","type":"CVE","created":"2026-11-06T01:52:09","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4539484200,"indicator":"CVE-2026-109619","type":"CVE","created